Falsifies `system_tests::user::revocation_in_flight_tests::a_capability_revoked_while_it_is_in_flight_does_not_reach_the_receiver`.

Architecture: aarch64, riscv64, x86_64

The defect is the tree as it stood until 2026-09-21: the patch removes the three lines this lane
added to `sched::delete_page_frame_caps_where`, which is the whole of the fix for the `PageFrame`
sweeps. Every revocation sweep then reads `Thread::capability_table` and nothing else, and a
capability parked in `Thread::outgoing_cap` by a `SEND_CAP` whose receiver has not arrived is in no
table, so it survives a whole-machine `PageFrame::REVOKE` and the next `RECEIVE_CAP` files it in the
receiver's own table.

This was the live tree, not an invented break. It was found by risk 7's adversarial pass asking the
question the brief names, "what would have to be true for a revocation claim to be false", against
every authority this kernel holds outside a capability slot.

Expected to fail: `assert!(!matches!(delivered, Some(c) if c.object == Object::PageFrame(phys,
page_frame_run_len(1))), "a revoked PageFrame capability was delivered out of the hand-off slot
...")`. Confirmed red on 2026-09-21, on aarch64, before the fix existed.

**The two assertions above the headline stay green under this patch**, which is what makes the red
mean the claim rather than the fixture. The vacuity guard (`rendezvous_waiting_senders(ep) == 1`)
says a capability really was in flight; the premise check (`!SENDER_STILL_HELD`) says the revoke
really did reach the one holder a table sweep can see. A revoke that did nothing at all would fail
the second one with a message saying the test proved nothing.

**Nothing here is architecture-specific.** `outgoing_cap`, the sweeps and the rendezvous are portable scheduler code, which is why the record names all three ISAs. It was aarch64's alone until milestone 323 (the falsification record is incomplete in five ways) replayed it on the other two.

**Two sibling sweeps carry the same fix and no record.**
`sched::delete_device_frame_caps_from_others` and `sched::delete_port_range_caps_impl` (`x86_64`
only) each gained the same two lines, and no test drives either through a parked hand-off. They are
reasoned from the code, which is the grade `notes/confinement-claims.md` already asks a reader to
apply to an unmeasured verdict.

Replayed on all three ISAs. The hunk is portable code, so one patch serves every leg. On 2026-10-03 (UTC), `cargo xtask test --arch riscv64 --test a_capability_revoked_while_it_is_in_flight_does_not_reach_the_receiver` and the same with `--arch x86_64` both came back red at `system_tests/src/user/revocation_in_flight_tests.rs:134`, the assertion this record predicts, which is where aarch64 was already red.

Regenerated 2026-10-04 (UTC) by the lane for the proposal "capability lookup off the global lock",
which moved each thread's capability table out of `Thread` and behind its own lock: the sweep now
iterates `(thread, table)` pairs, so the old hunk no longer matched. Same removal, same defect; the
added `let _ = t;` only keeps the patched build free of an unused-variable warning.
Replayed red on riscv64 the same day at `revocation_in_flight_tests.rs:134`, the assertion predicted.

Regenerated 2026-10-11 (UTC) for milestone 840 (the scheduler file is split along its seams):
the same lines, moved out of `kernel/src/sched.rs` into `kernel/src/sched/capabilities.rs` unchanged, so the defect is
the same and only the path and line numbers differ.

diff --git a/kernel/src/sched/capabilities.rs b/kernel/src/sched/capabilities.rs
index cd75c6fdd..63133422a 100644
--- a/kernel/src/sched/capabilities.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/sched/capabilities.rs
@@ -94,9 +94,7 @@ pub(super) fn delete_page_frame_caps_where(matches: impl Fn(&crate::cap::Object)
     };
     for (t, capabilities) in sched.threads.iter_mut_with_capabilities() {
         capabilities.lock().delete_matching(&matches);
-        if matches!(t.outgoing_capability, Some(c) if matches(&c.object)) {
-            t.outgoing_capability = None;
-        }
+        let _ = t;
     }
 }
 
